The regression results show that WFS has a statistically significant effect on the contact angle, as the associated  p -value is below 0.05, confirming its importance in the model. The negative coefficient (− 0.0095) indicates an inverse relationship between WFS and contact angle: as WFS increases, the contact angle decreases. This behavior is consistent with the physical phenomenon described in the literature, where higher wire feed rates produce taller and narrower beads, thereby increasing the aspect ratio and reducing the wetting angle [ 10 ]. Thus, the model confirms the tendency reported in previous studies, showing that increasing WFS promotes bead formation with greater height and lower contact angles, consistent with the expected geometric behavior. The goodness of fit is demonstrated by a pseudo-R 2  of 0.9670, indicating that the model explains 96.7% of the variability in the contact angle. This high value reflects the model’s excellent predictive capability, showing that it accurately captures the relationship between the analyzed variables. 3 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
